+## Event Snapshot
+
+The Riverbend Neighborhood Preparedness Workshop is scheduled for Saturday, October 25, 2025, from 10:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. in the Riverbend Library Community Room, 214 Cedar Avenue. The team should plan for 54 registered residents. The workshop’s purpose is to give residents practical preparedness guidance, connect neighbors, and leave each breakout group with concrete actions for the neighborhood map.
+
+Doors open at 9:45 a.m. The team will operate from the check-in point inside the east-side entrance, with the Community Room arranged for a whole-group presentation followed by three breakout groups. Organizer setup begins at 9:15 a.m.; teardown begins promptly at 2:00 p.m.
+
+## Run of Show
+
+| Time | Activity | Operating detail |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 9:15 a.m. | Organizer setup | Luis completes the room and AV checks while Maya prepares check-in materials; Priya checks the accessible route and reserved seating, and Theo confirms the lunch delivery plan. |
+| 9:45 a.m. | Doors and check-in | Maya checks in residents and holds the registration list and emergency contact sheet. The team directs arrivals from the east-side entrance to the Community Room. |
+| 10:00 a.m. | Welcome and safety note | Maya opens the workshop, identifies exits and the accessible restroom, introduces the team, and explains the day’s purpose. |
+| 10:15 a.m. | Utility shutoff demonstration | Luis leads the demonstration using the presentation setup and takes amplified questions. |
+| 11:00 a.m. | Three facilitated breakout groups | Groups cover household readiness, neighbor check-ins, and local resource mapping. Each group names one reporter and records two next actions for the later mapping session. Dana gives every group a five-minute warning. |
+| 12:30 p.m. | Lunch | Theo opens the lunch station, keeps labeled meals separated, and resolves meal questions. |
+| 1:10 p.m. | Neighborhood action mapping | The three reporters share their two next actions, and Maya guides the group in placing owners and next steps on the neighborhood map. |
+| 1:45 p.m. | Commitments and closing | Dana keeps time while Maya confirms owners, immediate follow-ups, and the closing message. |
+| 2:00 p.m. | Teardown | Luis leads equipment shutdown and room reset; the rest of the team clears check-in, food, and workshop materials. |
+
+The microphone remains in use for every presentation, report-out, and audience question. Dana’s time cues should keep lunch and action mapping on schedule while protecting every other agenda start time.
+
+## Roles and Contacts
+
+- **Maya Chen — event lead:** Owns check-in and on-site decisions, keeps the registration list and emergency contact sheet at the check-in desk, opens and closes the program, and facilitates neighborhood action mapping. Urgent operational questions go to Maya at check-in.
+- **Luis Ortega — room setup and AV:** Arranges the room and, before doors open, tests the projector, two handheld microphones, and presentation laptop. He also leads the utility shutoff demonstration and handles equipment shutdown during teardown.
+- **Priya Shah — accessibility lead:** Checks the step-free arrival route, seating, restroom access, handout formats, and microphone practice. Accessibility requests on the day go to Priya through the library front desk.
+- **Theo Brooks — catering liaison:** Confirms quantities and labeling, receives the order, sets up the lunch station, and keeps special meals identifiable. Meal issues go to Theo at the lunch station.
+- **Dana Cole — timekeeper:** Keeps the breakouts and closing on schedule and gives each breakout a five-minute warning before the 1:10 p.m. report-out and action-mapping transition.
+
+At the organizer huddle, each owner confirms readiness to Maya. Staff should route questions directly to the named owner so check-in, accessibility, AV, and catering issues can be resolved without interrupting the program.
+
+## Accessibility and Dietary Plan
+
+The step-free entrance is on the east side of the library and has automatic doors. The Community Room and an accessible restroom are on the same floor. Priya will inspect that route before 9:45 a.m., keep it unobstructed, reserve front-row seating, and make sure directional guidance is clear at check-in.
+
+Use a microphone for every presentation and every audience question. Prepare six large-print handout sets and a plain-text digital copy available at check-in. Priya will confirm both formats during setup. Attendee communications should request a fragrance-free environment, and Maya should refer any individual access request to Priya.
+
+Theo will receive 54 boxed lunches: 36 vegetarian wraps and 18 chicken wraps, all served with salad and water. Eight of the vegetarian meals must also be gluten-free and separately labeled. The entire order must remain nut-free, and compostable serviceware must be supplied. Theo will verify the count, ingredients, gluten-free labels, and serviceware before accepting the delivery, place the separately labeled meals where they will not be mixed with the other boxes, and staff the lunch station at 12:30 p.m.
+
+## Communications
+
+- **Friday, October 17 — response deadline:** Collect final dietary and accessibility requests so Priya and Theo can reconcile them with seating, format, and meal preparations.
+- **Monday, October 20 — main attendee reminder:** Confirm Saturday, October 25; the Riverbend Library Community Room at 214 Cedar Avenue; doors at 9:45 a.m.; the 10:00 a.m. start; and the 2:00 p.m. finish. Direct attendees to the automatic-door entrance on the east side, say that street parking is limited, and note that the Cedar Avenue bus stop is nearby. Request a fragrance-free environment and remind recipients to raise any outstanding access or dietary needs immediately.
+- **Friday, October 24 — day-before reminder:** Repeat the date, location, east-side entrance, doors-open time, event window, limited street-parking notice, and nearby bus stop. Keep this message short while retaining the access and fragrance-free reminders.
+
+The event team will use the routing already established for replies and day-of needs: operational questions to Maya at check-in, accessibility requests to Priya through the library front desk, and meal issues to Theo at the lunch station. No attendee communication should promise on-site parking.
